Skip the air-change event object when it cannot change anything.

Entity#setAirSupply allocated an EntityAirChangeEvent before checking this.valid, so the event was
built even during worldgen where it is deliberately never fired. With no listener registered the
event can neither cancel the change nor rewrite the amount, so the outcome is always
entityData.set(supply); taking that directly is exactly equivalent.

The method runs once per tick for anything drowning or refilling its air after leaving water, so
mobs pathing through rivers and oceans hit it continuously.

Behaviour is unchanged when a listener exists: the event is built, fired, and both the cancel path
(including the desync resend for players) and the rewritten amount work as before.

--- a/leaf-server/src/minecraft/java/net/minecraft/world/entity/Entity.java
+++ b/leaf-server/src/minecraft/java/net/minecraft/world/entity/Entity.java
@@ -4026,6 +4026,16 @@
     public void setAirSupply(final int supply) {
+        // AtlasSpigot start - fast path when the event cannot change anything
+        if (!this.valid || org.bukkit.event.entity.EntityAirChangeEvent.getHandlerList().getRegisteredListeners().length == 0) {
+            this.entityData.set(DATA_AIR_SUPPLY_ID, supply);
+            return;
+        }
+        // AtlasSpigot end
         // CraftBukkit start
         org.bukkit.event.entity.EntityAirChangeEvent event = new org.bukkit.event.entity.EntityAirChangeEvent(this.getBukkitEntity(), supply);
